# Citation metadata. This is the file Zenodo reads when a release is archived and
# the file GitHub renders as "Cite this repository". .mailmap only normalises how
# git reports history; it is not read here.
#
# BEFORE MINTING A DOI - two fields are deliberately provisional, because a
# Zenodo deposition is an immutable archive and whatever stands here is preserved
# permanently:
#
#   1. The author is recorded as an entity (the handle). For a scholarly record
#      it should be a person: replace the `- name:` entry with
#          - given-names: ...
#            family-names: ...
#            email: big@prod.codes
#            orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0000-0000-0000"
#      using the name that is to stand in the citation record for good.
#   2. ORCID is required in practice by SoftwareX and is free to obtain. Add it
#      above at the same time.
#
# Neither is invented here on the author's behalf. Both are one edit away.

cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: "KYB — Know Your Business"
abstract: >-
  A git-backed, searchable knowledge base and incident tracker for fleets of AI
  agents. Every entry is a markdown file and every change a commit, so superseded
  versions stay retrievable and searchable rather than being overwritten. Search
  is hybrid: BM25 fused by reciprocal rank with a multilingual embedding model
  running locally on CPU.
type: software
authors:
  - name: "alex09x"
    email: big@prod.codes
repository-code: "https://github.com/alex09x/kyb"
url: "https://kybmemory.com"
license: MIT
version: 0.2.1
keywords:
  - agent memory
  - knowledge base
  - incident tracking
  - hybrid search
  - information retrieval
  - provenance
  - version control
  - rust
